---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Icon from '../../components/Icon.astro';
import Katex from '../../components/Katex.astro';
import '../../styles/deepDive.css';
import 'katex/dist/katex.min.css';

const title = "Heritability Estimation: From Twin Models to Genomic REML (GREML / GCTA)";
const description = "A first-principles mathematical derivation of broad-sense vs narrow-sense heritability, Falconer's twin formulas, the missing heritability paradox, the realized Genomic Relatedness Matrix (GRM), AI-REML variance component estimation, and Lee et al. liability-scale transformation.";
/* Derived from this page's own content, not guessed: prose at 200 wpm, plus 15 s per
   display equation and 3 s per inline one. These constants had drifted to ~2.5x the
   truth (this page: 846 prose words, 9 display and 50 inline equations). Part 2 moves
   the series into a content collection where this is computed, not written down. */
const readingTime = "9 min read";
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} type="article">
  <article class="deep-dive-article">
    <!-- Back to Master Guide -->
    <nav style="margin-bottom: var(--space-4);">
      <a href="/deep_dives/statistical-genetics/" style="display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.35rem; font-size: var(--fs-small); color: var(--color-accent); font-weight: 600;">
        <Icon name="arrow-left" size={14} /> Back to Statistical Genetics Master Guide
      </a>
    </nav>

    <!-- Header -->
    <header class="deep-dive-header">
      <div class="deep-dive-meta-bar" style="margin-bottom: var(--space-2);">
        <span class="deep-dive-tag">Statistical Genetics · Part 3</span>
        <span class="deep-dive-level-badge"><Icon name="book-open" size={14} /> Advanced & Mathematical</span>
        <span class="deep-dive-time-badge"><Icon name="clock" size={14} /> {readingTime}</span>
      </div>
      <h1 class="deep-dive-title">{title}</h1>
      <p class="deep-dive-meta-bar">
        <span>By <strong>Kuan-Hao Chao</strong></span>
        <span>·</span>
        <span>Published August 2026</span>
      </p>
    </header>

    <!-- Lead / Introduction -->
    <p class="deep-dive-lead">
      How much of human phenotypic variation is attributable to inherited DNA differences rather than environmental exposures? Why did early GWAS fail to account for the heritability estimated in twin studies, creating the famous <strong>"Missing Heritability"</strong> paradox? This deep dive explores the mathematical machinery of heritability: classical <strong>Falconer Twin ACE models</strong>, the derivation of the <strong>realized Genomic Relatedness Matrix (GRM)</strong>, <strong>Genomic-REML (GREML)</strong> variance component estimation via <strong>GCTA</strong>, and the <strong>Lee et al. liability-scale transformation</strong> for ascertainment-biased case-control studies.
    </p>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="deep-dive-toc">
      <div class="deep-dive-toc-title">
        <Icon name="list" size={14} /> Table of Contents
      </div>
      <ol class="deep-dive-toc-list">
        <li><a href="#definitions">1. Heritability Formulations: Broad-Sense vs. Narrow-Sense</a></li>
        <li><a href="#twin-ace">2. Classical Twin Studies & Falconer's ACE Model</a></li>
        <li><a href="#missing">3. The "Missing Heritability" Paradox</a></li>
        <li><a href="#grm">4. The Realized Genomic Relatedness Matrix (GRM)</a></li>
        <li><a href="#greml">5. Linear Mixed Model & Genomic REML (GREML)</a></li>
        <li><a href="#ai-reml">6. The Average Information REML (AI-REML) Algorithm</a></li>
        <li><a href="#liability">7. Lee et al. (2011) Liability-Scale Transformation</a></li>
        <li><a href="#summary">8. Summary & Next in the Statistical Genetics Series</a></li>
      </ol>
    </div>

    <!-- Chapter 1 -->
    <section id="definitions">
      <h2>1. Heritability Formulations: Broad-Sense vs. Narrow-Sense</h2>
      <p>
        Heritability is a population-specific parameter measuring the proportion of total phenotypic variance (<Katex tex="V_P" />) attributable to genetic factors:
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">Broad-Sense (H²) vs. Narrow-Sense (h²) Heritability</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="H^2 = \frac{V_G}{V_P} = \frac{V_A + V_D + V_I}{V_P}, \qquad h^2 = \frac{V_A}{V_P}" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-legend">
          <Katex tex="V_A" /> = Additive genetic variance &emsp;|&emsp;
          <Katex tex="V_D" /> = Dominance variance &emsp;|&emsp;
          <Katex tex="V_I" /> = Epistatic variance.
        </div>
      </div>

      <p>
        <strong>Narrow-sense heritability (<Katex tex="h^2" />)</strong> is of primary biological and statistical importance because only additive allele substitution effects are transmitted linearly from parents to offspring.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 2 -->
    <section id="twin-ace">
      <h2>2. Classical Twin Studies & Falconer's ACE Model</h2>
      <p>
        Classical quantitative genetics leverages the natural experiment of Monozygotic (MZ) twins (sharing 100% of genomic alleles, <Katex tex="\Phi_{\text{MZ}} = 0.5" />) and Dizygotic (DZ) twins (sharing on average 50% of segregating alleles, <Katex tex="\Phi_{\text{DZ}} = 0.25" />).
      </p>

      <h3>The ACE Structural Equation Model</h3>
      <p>
        The ACE model decomposes phenotypic variance into:
        <br/>
        &bull; <strong><Katex tex="A" /> (Additive Genetic Variance):</strong> <Katex tex="a^2 = V_A / V_P" />.
        <br/>
        &bull; <strong><Katex tex="C" /> (Common / Shared Environment):</strong> <Katex tex="c^2 = V_C / V_P" /> (reared in the same household, same parental socioeconomic status).
        <br/>
        &bull; <strong><Katex tex="E" /> (Unique / Non-Shared Environment):</strong> <Katex tex="e^2 = V_E / V_P" /> (individual-specific environmental exposures and measurement error).
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">Falconer's Twin Formulas (1960)</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="r_{\text{MZ}} = a^2 + c^2, \quad r_{\text{DZ}} = \frac{1}{2}a^2 + c^2 \implies a^2 = h^2 = 2(r_{\text{MZ}} - r_{\text{DZ}}), \quad c^2 = 2r_{\text{DZ}} - r_{\text{MZ}}, \quad e^2 = 1 - r_{\text{MZ}}" display={true} />
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 3 -->
    <section id="missing">
      <h2>3. The "Missing Heritability" Paradox</h2>
      <p>
        Between 2007 and 2010, early Genome-Wide Association Studies (GWAS) for complex human traits (e.g. height, BMI, Type 2 Diabetes, Schizophrenia) yielded a profound paradox:
        <br/>
        &bull; Twin studies estimated human height heritability at <Katex tex="h^2_{\text{twin}} \approx 80\%" />.
        <br/>
        &bull; However, summing the variance explained by all individual SNPs reaching genome-wide significance (<Katex tex="P < 5 \times 10^{-8}" />) accounted for only <Katex tex="\approx 5\%" /> of the variance!
      </p>
      <p>
        This massive gap (<Katex tex="80\% \text{ vs. } 5\%" />) was termed the <strong>"Missing Heritability" problem</strong>. Speculations ranged from undiscovered structural variants, rare mutations of large effect, epistatic interactions, to inflated twin estimates due to shared environmental confounding.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 4 -->
    <section id="grm">
      <h2>4. The Realized Genomic Relatedness Matrix (GRM)</h2>
      <p>
        In 2010, <strong>Jian Yang, Peter Visscher, and colleagues (GCTA)</strong> published a breakthrough resolution: the missing heritability was not absent—it was scattered across hundreds of thousands of common SNPs whose individual effect sizes were too small to clear the stringent <Katex tex="5 \times 10^{-8}" /> significance threshold in modest sample sizes.
      </p>
      <p>
        Instead of testing one SNP at a time, Yang et al. realized they could estimate the cumulative variance tagged by all SNPs simultaneously by calculating pairwise genetic similarity across nominally "unrelated" individuals using the <strong>Genomic Relatedness Matrix (GRM)</strong> <Katex tex="\mathbf{A}" />.
      </p>

      <h3>VanRaden (2008) / Yang et al. (2010) GRM Estimator</h3>
      <p>
        Let <Katex tex="X_{ij} \in \{0, 1, 2\}" /> be the genotype dosage of individual <Katex tex="i" /> at SNP <Katex tex="j" />, and <Katex tex="p_j" /> be the minor allele frequency. The standardized genotype matrix <Katex tex="\mathbf{W}" /> is defined by:
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">Standardized Genotype Matrix & GRM Formula</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="W_{ij} = \frac{X_{ij} - 2p_j}{\sqrt{2p_j(1 - p_j)}}, \qquad \mathbf{A} = \frac{1}{M} \mathbf{W}\mathbf{W}^T \iff A_{ik} = \frac{1}{M} \sum_{j=1}^{M} \frac{(X_{ij} - 2p_j)(X_{kj} - 2p_j)}{2p_j(1 - p_j)}" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-legend">
          <Katex tex="A_{ii} \approx 1 + F_i" /> (diagonal elements measure individual inbreeding coefficient) &emsp;|&emsp;
          <Katex tex="A_{ik}" /> (off-diagonal elements measure genetic similarity relative to base population).
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 5 -->
    <section id="greml">
      <h2>5. Linear Mixed Model & Genomic REML (GREML)</h2>
      <p>
        GREML treats the collective polygenic effects of all <Katex tex="M" /> SNPs as a random effect vector in a Linear Mixed Model (LMM):
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">GREML Linear Mixed Model Formulation</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="\mathbf{y} = \mathbf{X}\boldsymbol{\beta} + \mathbf{g} + \boldsymbol{\epsilon}, \quad \mathbf{g} \sim \mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0}, \sigma_g^2 \mathbf{A}), \quad \boldsymbol{\epsilon} \sim \mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0}, \sigma_e^2 \mathbf{I})" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-legend">
          <Katex tex="\mathbf{y}" /> = <Katex tex="N \times 1" /> vector of continuous phenotypes &emsp;|&emsp;
          <Katex tex="\mathbf{X}\boldsymbol{\beta}" /> = Fixed-effect covariates (age, sex, top ancestry PCs) &emsp;|&emsp;
          <Katex tex="\mathbf{g} = \mathbf{W}\mathbf{u}" /> = Polygenic breeding value vector with <Katex tex="\mathbf{u} \sim \mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0}, \frac{\sigma_g^2}{M}\mathbf{I})" />.
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>Total Phenotypic Covariance Matrix</h3>
      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">Phenotypic Covariance & SNP Heritability</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="\mathbf{V} = \operatorname{Var}(\mathbf{y}) = \sigma_g^2 \mathbf{A} + \sigma_e^2 \mathbf{I}, \qquad h^2_{\text{SNP}} = \frac{\sigma_g^2}{\sigma_g^2 + \sigma_e^2}" display={true} />
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 6 -->
    <section id="ai-reml">
      <h2>6. The Average Information REML (AI-REML) Algorithm</h2>
      <p>
        Ordinary Maximum Likelihood (ML) underestimates variance components because it fails to account for degrees of freedom lost in estimating fixed effects <Katex tex="\boldsymbol{\beta}" />. <strong>Restricted Maximum Likelihood (REML, Patterson & Thompson 1971)</strong> projects the phenotype vector onto the null space of fixed effects via the projection matrix <Katex tex="\mathbf{P}" />:
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">REML Projection Matrix & Restricted Log-Likelihood</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="\mathbf{P} = \mathbf{V}^{-1} - \mathbf{V}^{-1} \mathbf{X} \left(\mathbf{X}^T \mathbf{V}^{-1} \mathbf{X}\right)^{-1} \mathbf{X}^T \mathbf{V}^{-1}" display={true} />
          <Katex tex="\ln L_R = -\frac{1}{2} \left[ \ln |\mathbf{V}| + \ln |\mathbf{X}^T \mathbf{V}^{-1} \mathbf{X}| + \mathbf{y}^T \mathbf{P} \mathbf{y} \right]" display={true} />
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>Average Information Fisher Scoring</h3>
      <p>
        Computing the exact Hessian matrix requires calculating <Katex tex="\operatorname{tr}(\mathbf{P}\mathbf{A}\mathbf{P}\mathbf{A})" />, which is cubic in sample size (<Katex tex="\mathcal{O}(N^3)" />). The <strong>AI-REML algorithm (Gilmour et al. 1995)</strong> computes the average of the Observed Fisher Information and Expected Fisher Information matrices:
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">Average Information Matrix (AI)</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="\text{AI}(\sigma_i^2, \sigma_j^2) = \frac{1}{2} \mathbf{y}^T \mathbf{P} \frac{\partial \mathbf{V}}{\partial \sigma_i^2} \mathbf{P} \frac{\partial \mathbf{V}}{\partial \sigma_j^2} \mathbf{P} \mathbf{y}" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-legend">
          AI-REML replaces heavy matrix trace inversions with quadratic forms involving vector-matrix multiplications, accelerating GREML convergence by orders of magnitude.
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 7 -->
    <section id="liability">
      <h2>7. Lee et al. (2011) Liability-Scale Transformation</h2>
      <p>
        In disease case-control studies, cases are heavily oversampled relative to general population prevalence (e.g. 50% cases in a cohort vs 1% population prevalence). Fitting a linear GREML model directly to binary 0/1 indicator phenotypes produces an <strong>observed-scale heritability</strong> (<Katex tex="h^2_o" />) that is distorted by sample ascertainment.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Lee, Wray, Goddard, and Visscher (2011)</strong> derived the exact mathematical transformation to convert <Katex tex="h^2_o" /> to the true population <strong>liability-scale heritability</strong> (<Katex tex="h^2_l" />):
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">Lee et al. (2011) Liability Conversion Formula</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="h^2_l = h^2_o \frac{K^2(1 - K)^2}{z_K^2 P(1 - P)}" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-legend">
          <Katex tex="h^2_o" /> = Observed-scale heritability estimated by GREML on 0/1 disease coding &emsp;|&emsp;
          <Katex tex="K" /> = Population disease prevalence &emsp;|&emsp;
          <Katex tex="P" /> = Proportion of cases in the GWAS sample (<Katex tex="N_{\text{cases}} / N_{\text{total}}" />) &emsp;|&emsp;
          <Katex tex="z_K = \phi(\Phi^{-1}(1 - K))" /> = Standard normal probability density height at the disease liability threshold.
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Summary -->
    <section id="summary">
      <h2>8. Summary & Next in the Statistical Genetics Series</h2>
      <div class="deep-dive-callout">
        <div class="deep-dive-callout-title">
          <Icon name="check-circle" size={15} /> Key Mathematical Takeaways
        </div>
        <p>
          &bull; <strong>Falconer's Twin Model:</strong> Estimates <Katex tex="h^2 = 2(r_{\text{MZ}} - r_{\text{DZ}})" /> and shared environment <Katex tex="c^2 = 2r_{\text{DZ}} - r_{\text{MZ}}" />.
          <br/>
          &bull; <strong>Missing Heritability Solved:</strong> Yang et al. (2010) proved polygenic variance is tagged by thousands of small-effect common SNPs.
          <br/>
          &bull; <strong>GRM Construction:</strong> <Katex tex="\mathbf{A} = \frac{1}{M}\mathbf{W}\mathbf{W}^T" /> quantifies empirical relatedness among nominally unrelated individuals.
          <br/>
          &bull; <strong>AI-REML:</strong> Efficiently estimates variance components <Katex tex="\sigma_g^2" /> and <Katex tex="\sigma_e^2" /> without full Hessian inversion.
          <br/>
          &bull; <strong>Liability Transformation:</strong> <Katex tex="h^2_l = h^2_o \frac{K^2(1-K)^2}{z_K^2 P(1-P)}" /> corrects for case-control ascertainment bias.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="algo-promo-card" style="margin-top: 1.25rem;">
        <h3>📖 Next Deep Dive: LD Score Regression (LDSC & S-LDSC)</h3>
        <p>
          Continue to Part 4 to discover how to estimate SNP heritability, detect population stratification confounding, and partition tissue-specific enrichments using GWAS summary statistics alone.
        </p>
        <a href="/deep_dives/statgen-ldsc-sldsc/" class="algo-promo-btn">
          <span>Continue to Part 4: LD Score Regression</span>
          <Icon name="arrow-right" size={15} />
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
